-- Individualize demo payment_transactions per-customer timing (backlog D0c addendum).
--
-- WHAT: replaces the 59 demo customers' payment_transactions with per-customer
-- individualized days_early_late/days_to_pay sequences, instead of the 4 fixed
-- persona templates every customer in a bucket previously shared verbatim
-- (27 healthy / 10 watch / 22 at_risk, all sharing one of exactly 4 identical
-- 8-value sequences -- see 20260605130000_b0_phase4g_payment_realism.sql).
--
-- WHY: found 2026-09-29 while building the new /payments page -- with a
-- dedicated per-customer table, the duplication became immediately visible:
-- dozens of unrelated customers showing byte-identical Last-30d-avg /
-- Prior-30d-avg / Change / On-time values to one decimal place. Already
-- logged as a lower-priority realism gap in D0c's 2026-09-23 addendum;
-- promoted to a real fix once it was user-visible on a live page rather than
-- buried in raw transaction rows.
--
-- HOW: each customer keeps its original amount_paid values (unchanged) and
-- persona bucket (healthy/watch/at_risk), but gets its own randomly-seeded
-- (seeded on customer_id, so deterministic and reproducible) days_early_late
-- sequence. Generated and verified offline against the REAL
-- analyse-payment-behaviour skill logic (amount-weighted trend/health,
-- ported 1:1 for generation) before this file was written: all 59 customers
-- compute to their intended persona health (tests/payments/intended.json),
-- and all 59 sequences are pairwise distinct. Re-verify after applying with:
--   npx tsx tests/payments/verify-payments.mjs
--
-- Dates and demo_days_offset follow the same self-healing pattern as
-- 20260925000002_payment_date_auto_reanchor.sql: payment_date is written as
-- CURRENT_DATE minus (or, for a couple of very-early-paying healthy
-- customers, plus) a fixed day-count -- so it's correct whenever this
-- migration actually runs -- invoice_date/invoice_due_date are derived from
-- it via each row's own days_to_pay/days_early_late, and demo_days_offset is
-- set explicitly so fn_reset_demo_payment_dates() keeps re-anchoring these
-- rows correctly on every future demo reset without another one-time fix.
--
-- Net terms per persona bucket (Net 30 for healthy/at_risk, Net 45 for
-- watch) matches the net_terms implied by the original migration's
-- (days_to_pay - days_early_late) for that bucket -- unchanged from before.
--
-- NOTE on the live payment-behaviour-agent's current-vs-prior-30-day-window
-- comparison (analyse-payment-trend.ts): with monthly-cadence transactions,
-- each 30-day window only ever contains a single payment, so
-- PAYMENT_VOLATILITY (population stddev of the CURRENT window) can never
-- fire regardless of how volatile a customer's history looks -- this is a
-- separate, pre-existing structural gap in the cadence/windowing design,
-- not something this migration changes or fixes. Logged separately.
--
-- Scope: payment_transactions timing columns only. amount_paid, invoices,
-- customers.current_exposure, and customers.net_terms are untouched.


-- Scoped to exactly the 59 customers reinserted below -- NOT a blanket
-- is_demo=true delete, which on staging would also wipe the ~23
-- separately-seeded staging-only customers noted in D0c that this
-- migration does not touch or reinsert.
